WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.477
Go makes errors explicit, which is useful
for security review.

2
00:00:03.557 --> 00:00:07.264
Each returned error is a point where the
application decides whether to retry,

3
00:00:07.344 --> 00:00:09.815
stop, clean up, roll back, log, or return
a response.

4
00:00:09.895 --> 00:00:12.591
Secure error handling preserves invariants
on failure.

5
00:00:12.671 --> 00:00:16.602
It avoids partial state changes that leave
a request half-complete or a resource in

6
00:00:16.682 --> 00:00:17.525
an unclear state.

7
00:00:17.605 --> 00:00:20.244
Internal errors can include useful
technical context,

8
00:00:20.324 --> 00:00:23.076
but user-facing responses should be safe
and consistent.

9
00:00:23.156 --> 00:00:26.751
A client usually needs to know whether a
request was invalid, unauthorized,

10
00:00:26.831 --> 00:00:30.032
not found, conflicted, rate limited, or
temporarily unavailable.

11
00:00:30.112 --> 00:00:33.257
The client does not need raw stack traces,
secret values, SQL text,

12
00:00:33.337 --> 00:00:36.595
internal file paths, dependency details,
or configuration values.

13
00:00:36.675 --> 00:00:39.932
Logging should support investigation
without becoming a data leak.

14
00:00:40.012 --> 00:00:43.214
Structured logs, request IDs, correlation
IDs, event categories,

15
00:00:43.294 --> 00:00:46.607
and appropriate log levels help operators
understand what happened.

16
00:00:46.687 --> 00:00:50.226
At the same time, tokens, cookies, API
keys, passwords, private user data,

17
00:00:50.306 --> 00:00:53.788
raw payloads, and internal configuration
should not be logged casually.

18
00:00:53.868 --> 00:00:57.125
Logs often flow to dashboards, tickets,
alerts, long-term storage,

19
00:00:57.205 --> 00:00:59.115
and third-party monitoring platforms.

20
00:00:59.195 --> 00:01:01.722
Secret protection should be boring and
disciplined.

21
00:01:01.802 --> 00:01:03.094
Keep secrets server-side.

22
00:01:03.174 --> 00:01:06.319
Read them from approved environment or
secret-store mechanisms.

23
00:01:06.399 --> 00:01:08.702
Avoid printing secrets during startup or
tests.

24
00:01:08.782 --> 00:01:10.635
Scope credentials to the job they need.

25
00:01:10.715 --> 00:01:12.850
When errors and logs are designed
carefully,

26
00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:16.861
teams get useful visibility while reducing
the chance that observability becomes

27
00:01:16.941 --> 00:01:18.064
another exposure path.

28
00:01:18.144 --> 00:01:20.897
The safest error strategy is intentional
and consistent.

29
00:01:20.977 --> 00:01:24.683
Decide which errors should be retried,
which should be reported to the caller,

30
00:01:24.763 --> 00:01:26.167
which should trigger alerts,

31
00:01:26.247 --> 00:01:29.056
and which should be summarized without
sensitive details.

32
00:01:29.136 --> 00:01:30.203
Then test those paths.

33
00:01:30.283 --> 00:01:34.158
Error handling is part of the
application's security contract because failure is

34
00:01:34.238 --> 00:01:36.260
when assumptions are most likely to break.
